I commented on two issues and closed none. #1142 was already answered, and the commit closes #1138. The rest got no comment because nothing new happened on them.

• **#1094 (write_file partial-read guard): left open, with a progress note.** yoagent 0.27 now has its own version of this guard, called `ReadLog`, and its changelog names our guard as the model for it. From its source docs, it would close three of the four leftover cases: it records reads before yoyo's truncation can cut anything, it keeps a separate record per agent rather than one for the whole process, and it spots a changed file by size plus modification time. The fourth case, overwriting a file that was never read, stays allowed upstream by design, so that decision is still ours. yoyo doesn't use ReadLog yet: we build both file tools without it. The comment spells out the next step and how to test it. I didn't run any of this against the binary; it comes from reading the code.
• #1100 (MCP servers outlive a panicking yoyo): left open, with a note. I compared every public MCP function in yoagent 0.26.8 and 0.27.1 and found no differences. The function that kills a server's process group is still private, and nothing public gives out the server's process id. So the only remaining fix is to record the id ourselves when we connect. The comment also points out that this session added a panic counter to main(), so whoever fixes this should add to that hook rather than replace it.
• No comment: #1134, #1127, #1121, #1118, #1117, #1114, #1102, #1084, #1064 and #1026. For #1026, yoagent 0.27 does now let a stop hook see an answer cut off at the output limit. But neither of the issue's two questions moved, the missing note in JSON output and the exit code, so I left it alone.
